} - 1$ for all $n ∈ N$." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186083595 1074 "To prove a statement using strong induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={2}^{n}-1\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base cases. Since the first application of the recurrence formula ${a}_{n+1}=3{a}_{n}-2{a}_{n-1}$ does not apply until ${a}_{3},$ we must check the base cases $P(1)$ and $P(2).$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove our statement is true for the base cases $n=1$ and $n=2.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & ={2}^{1}-1 \\\\ {a}_{2} & ={2}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base cases are true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the right-hand side and show that it equals the left-hand side in both cases.\n\nNext, we write down our (strong) **inductive hypothesis,** which is to assume that $P(n)$ is true *for all* $n\\le k,$ where $k\\ge 2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={2}^{n}-1$ is true for all $n\\le k$ for $k\\ge 2.$ In particular, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={2}^{k}-1 \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={2}^{k-1}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={2}^{k+1}-1.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${a}_{n}={2}^{n}-1$ for all $n\\in N.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186083909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186082355 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186083909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385480 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186083909 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186083909 1071 "Determine the value of the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{6}^{n}}$  rounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186083909 1071 "Determine the value of the series $∑_(n = 1)^(4)\\frac{1}{6^{n}}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186083909 1074 "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{6}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{6}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{1}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6},r=\\frac{1}{6}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{n} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot [1-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{4}] \\\\  & ≈0.200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to three decimal places." 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186092318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385164 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186096763 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382935 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186096815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385262 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186100823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186384662 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186100884 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383444 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385173 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385176 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385486 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186115255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383292 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186115256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383285 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186115256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385605 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186119671 1071 "Given that $\\det(X)=\\det(Y),$ which elementary row operations transforms matrix $X$ to matrix $Y,$ where\n\n![](images/36/3692f985d592f9680ca9fbf38991df32c7665ae02c7dae04fec6062e3b62884d.png)" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186119671 1071 "Given that $\\det(X)=\\det(Y),$ which elementary row operations transforms matrix $X$ to matrix $Y,$ where\n\n![](images/fd/fd6c1665506e5186c1ccac2cf669040943bbc31347b27fae1c77ffe9b01ed542.png)" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186119781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385428 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186119781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385563 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186119782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383173 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186119782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383180 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186119782 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383187 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186119783 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383208 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186119783 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383215 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186119783 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385435 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186119783 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385570 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186119783 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385577 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186119856 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383222 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186119856 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385448 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186119857 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383194 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186119857 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383201 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186120458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118832 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186120458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385455 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186120458 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186120458 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $5 \\cdot 5$ matrix and $det(A) =-28$. The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_{5}↔R_{3}$ (switching the third and the fifth rows). What is the value of $det(B)$?" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186120458 1071 "Suppose $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=-28.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{5}↔{R}_{3}$ (switching the third and the fifth rows). What is the value of $det(B)?$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186120458 1074 "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =-det(A) \\\\  & =-(-28) \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186120461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118850 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186120461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385461 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186120461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186120461 1071 "Suppose $C$ is a $7 \\cdot 7$ matrix and $det(C) =-126$. The matrix $D$ is obtained from $C$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_{5}:=\\frac{8}{21}R_{5}$. What is the value of $det(D)$?" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186120461 1071 "Suppose $C$ is a $7\\times 7$ matrix and $det(C)=-126.$ The matrix $D$ is obtained from $C$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{5}:=\\frac{8}{21}{R}_{5}.$ What is the value of $det(D)?$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186120461 1074 "The row operation applied to $C$ consists of multiplying a row by the number $\\frac{8}{21}.$\n\nWhen we do this, the determinant is multiplied by the same number. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\frac{8}{21}det(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{21}\\cdot (-126) \\\\  & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186120462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186118856 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186120462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385467 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186120462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186120462 1071 "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(X) =-8$, find $det(Y)$.\n$X = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, Y = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186120462 1071 "Consider the matrices given below. Given that $X$ and $Y$ are row equivalent matrices and $det(X)=-8$, find $det(Y).$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186120462 1074 "Notice that $Y$ can be obtained from $X$ by swapping the $2$nd and the $3$rd rows. \n\nThis changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one.\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =-det(X) \\\\  & =-(-8) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383299 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383306 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383313 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385493 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383320 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124445 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385500 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124449 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383089 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124449 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385507 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383096 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383103 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385337 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385350 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385535 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124451 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385542 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124451 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385549 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124453 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383110 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124453 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385556 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124454 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383327 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124454 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385740 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124454 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385747 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383362 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385473 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124455 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385754 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383334 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383341 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383348 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124456 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383355 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124457 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383152 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124467 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383159 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124467 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383166 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124467 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385514 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124468 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385521 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124468 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385528 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124469 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383229 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124470 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383236 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124470 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383243 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124470 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385383 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124614 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385905 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124614 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385912 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124616 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383145 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122252 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186124947 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385344 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124947 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124947 1071 "Evaluate $∇h(x, y)$ at $(2, 3)$ if $h(x, y) = 4x^{2} - 3xy + 2y^{2} + y$." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186124947 1071 "Evaluate $∇h(x,y)$ at $(2,3)$ if $h(x,y)=4{x}^{2}-3xy+2{y}^{2}+y.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124947 1074 "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}-3xy+2{y}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =8x-3y+0+0 \\\\  & =8x-3y \\\\ {h}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}-3xy+2{y}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =0-3x+4y+1 \\\\  & =4y-3x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & ={h}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{h}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(8x-3y)\\text{ }i+(4y-3x+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇h(2,3) & =(8\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 3)\\text{ }i+(4\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 2+1)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =7\\text{ }i+7\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122462 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186124982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385357 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124982 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S$, defined by $z = f(x, y)$, with the plane $y = 1$. Given that the point $P(1, 1, 0)$ lies on $C$, and that $f_{x} = 3x + 6x\\ln (xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186124982 1071 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3x+6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186124982 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,1,0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)} & =3(1)+6(1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =3+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1)}(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =3(x-1) \\\\ z & =3x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186125005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122600 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186125005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385363 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186125005 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186125005 1071 "Suppose that a function $f(x, y, z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U$, and that\n\n$\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} = (xy^{2} + 2y)e^{xy} + 2z^{3}, \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂z} = 6xz^{2}$.\n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(1, 0, 1)$:\n\n$\\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x} + \\frac{∂^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186125005 1071 "Suppose that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}=(x{y}^{2}+2y){e}^{xy}+2{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}=6x{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186125005 1074 "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nHence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x{y}^{2}+2y){e}^{xy}+2{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =(x{y}^{2}+2y)\\cdot x{e}^{xy}+(2xy+2)\\cdot {e}^{xy}+0 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4xy+2){e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(6x{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =12xz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,0,1)} & =(0+0+2){e}^{0}=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,0,1)} & =12(1)(1)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂x∂y∂x}{|}_{(1,0,1)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂x∂z}{|}_{(1,0,1)} & =2+12=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186125080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123050 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186125080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385390 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186125080 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186125080 1071 "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\sin (xy) + y^{2}$ at the point $(0,-1, 1)$." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186125080 1071 "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}$ at the point $(0,-1,1).$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186125080 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2} \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+{y}^{2}-z)k \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2y)\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,-1,1) & =(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\text{ }i+[(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+2(-1)]\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-i-2\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{-1}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x}{-1}=\\frac{y+1}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{1} \\\\ x=\\frac{y+1}{2}=z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186125084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186123074 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186125084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385396 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186125084 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186125084 1071 "For the function $f(x, y, z) = xy + 5x + z^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x, y, z) = (1,-1, 2)$." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186125084 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+5x+{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2).$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186125084 1074 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy+5x+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(y+5)\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(-1+5)\\,i+(1)\\,j+2(2)\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,i+j+4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4\\,i+j+4\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,2).$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186128844 1071 "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](images/40/40212b3cc1bb25e4d42b96ba9fd2c1958772a32fe69136dca347be295956b9ea.png)\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where ![](images/de/de70592e2668d2ef6030843fe9e20dd3da9fc1edec2501e7cd318a57682f3f0e.png)" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186128844 1071 "For the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ defined as\n\n![](images/ee/eec66e1a6fdb16b157ec4ba8ea32b8e2328c26910594a7d21bb7346669797627.png)\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where ![](images/01/01df906c78b9ed5b4d6f1e29b4aeeb3be607b7570db357c4dce5949702432011.png)" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186128844 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/62/62dec67282c5bc2707377cb976412a48ca3c41ffeed6aa74ef0d5b1ef8d6e380.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}$ and $y(u,v) = v\\sin{u}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =-2v({sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u) \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when ![](images/72/72902f1a278bc1316ff58c54940b4d5e65fa04aa8e1711c8e0a1726f57cd3641.png) the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2.\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186128844 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/9b/9b0ee6271962e3ede406351f05d805ae60f4b37ce5a45489b117ab3786ed3c23.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v) = 2v\\cos{u}$ and $y(u,v) = v\\sin{u}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡u-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot v\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =-2v({sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u) \\\\  & =-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when ![](images/72/72902f1a278bc1316ff58c54940b4d5e65fa04aa8e1711c8e0a1726f57cd3641.png) the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}\\right|_{(0,-1)} = -2(-1) = 2.\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186128867 1071 "Sketch the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n![](images/0e/0e620d5ca71a48c87a0d5942ffa8378964f6172bff7396c290024a5299531204.png)\nis (locally) orientation-reversing." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186128867 1071 "Sketch the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by \n![](images/4c/4cb994a50c42c48bad095fb056406cdcebd42143b3c0ce041ad06f52a2942a01.png)\nis (locally) orientation-reversing." 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129003 1074 "The tangent plane to a surface is horizontal if and only if the normal vector to the tangent plane is vertical. So, let's first find the general expression of a normal vector to the surface.\n\nRemember that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0), \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0), -1 \\right\\rangle$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =2y+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n\\left\\langle y, \\: 2y+x, \\: -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe want to find where this normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/a2/a20e553b10de1d00abd5beead7766a1859073f07b1f5bfb734b51e8433fbeee0.png) \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2(0) +x = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz = 0^2+(0)(0)= 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186129003 1074 "The tangent plane to a surface is horizontal if and only if the normal vector to the tangent plane is vertical. So, let's first find the general expression of a normal vector to the surface.\n\nRemember that the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0)\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0), \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0), -1 \\right\\rangle$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =2y+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n\\left\\langle y, \\: 2y+x, \\: -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe want to find where this normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/df/df6542baabd72b24ce115cb79c912c7b19fd3bd1b663467956445553b60a68c1.png) \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2(0) +x = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz = 0^2+(0)(0)= 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383117 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383124 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383131 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383138 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385584 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129184 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385598 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129186 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385591 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383507 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383514 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385414 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385761 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383521 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129288 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383574 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129288 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383581 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383553 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126655 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186129531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186383535 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129531 1071 "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(1, 2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $δA$. Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T$, given by\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} + v \\\\ 2u - v^{3}\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186129531 1071 "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(1,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+v \\\\ 2u-{v}^{3}\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129531 1074 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3{v}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-3{v}^{2})-1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6u{v}^{2}-2 \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,2)} & =(-6u{v}^{2}-2)\\,{|}_{u=1\\,v=2} \\\\  & =-6(1)(2)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-26|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =26\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127159 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186129618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385402 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129618 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w = e^{x} + e^{y} - e^{z}$ at the point $(1, 1, 1, e)$." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186129618 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent hyperplane to the hypersurface $w={e}^{x}+{e}^{y}-{e}^{z}$ at the point $(1,1,1,e).$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129618 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the hypersurface $w=f(x,y,z)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0},{w}_{0})$ on the hypersurface can be written as\n\n$$\nw-{w}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})+(z-{z}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}-{e}^{z})={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}-{e}^{z})={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x}+{e}^{y}-{e}^{z})=-{e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,1) & ={e}^{1}=e \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,1) & ={e}^{1}=e \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,1) & =-{e}^{1}=-e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent hyperplane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w-e & =(x-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(1,1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂y}(1,1,1)+(z-1)\\frac{∂f}{∂z}(1,1,1) \\\\ w-e & =e(x-1)+e(y-1)-e(z-1) \\\\ w-e & =ex-e+ey-e-ez+e \\\\ -ex-ey+ez+w & =0 \\\\ ex+ey-ez-w & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186127237 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186129631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385408 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129631 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129631 1071 "At which points on the surface $z = \\ln (1 + x^{2} + y^{2})$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186129631 1071 "At which points on the surface $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is the tangent plane horizontal?" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186129631 1074 "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ while the second equation gives $y=0.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=0$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point where the tangent plane is horizontal is $(0,0,0).$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383040 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383250 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383257 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133883 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382853 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133883 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383264 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133883 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383271 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133883 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383278 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133883 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385919 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382832 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382839 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382846 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383047 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385421 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133904 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383068 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133904 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383075 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186133904 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383082 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130528 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186134067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186382722 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134067 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134067 1071 "![](images/34/3411e11060b06a11c82ad677f50731faa31c6bc2e83ccb0d0d10931cf3d76592.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F$?\n\n1. $divF = 0$\n2. $divF > 0$\n3. $divF < 0$\n4. $F$ is solenoidal" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186134067 1071 "![](images/34/3411e11060b06a11c82ad677f50731faa31c6bc2e83ccb0d0d10931cf3d76592.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134067 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P,$ then $F$ acts like a source at $P.$\n\n- If $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P,$ then $F$ acts like a sink at $P.$\n\n- If $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P,$ then $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vectors decrease as we move in the $y$-direction. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130564 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186134073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186382826 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134073 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134073 1071 "![](images/86/864ce19530f022e52b7c9513c0cebc64e898f6e71ecf1066ab6aad92d6df40bf.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F$?\n\n1. $divF = 0$\n2. $divF > 0$\n3. $divF < 0$\n4. $F$ is solenoidal" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186134073 1071 "![](images/86/864ce19530f022e52b7c9513c0cebc64e898f6e71ecf1066ab6aad92d6df40bf.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134073 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132457 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186134408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385442 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134408 1071 "Let $V = C[0, 1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0, 1]$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)dt$.\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t) = t + 2$." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186134408 1071 "Let $V=C[0,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=t+2.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134408 1074 "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{⟨f(t),f(t)⟩}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(t),f(t)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4t+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+2{t}^{2}+4t)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+2+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n‖f(t)‖=\\sqrt{\\frac{19}{3}}.\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133392 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186134564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385899 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134564 1071 "An inner product on $V = R^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x, y⟩ = x_{1}y_{1} + x_{2}y_{2} + 5x_{3}y_{3}$.\n\nNormalize the vector $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ under this inner product." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186134564 1071 "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is defined as $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+5{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ \n\nNormalize the  vector  $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under this inner product." 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186134564 1074 "The norm of a vector $v$ with respect to an inner product $⟨\\cdot ,\\cdot ⟩$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖=\\sqrt{⟨v,v⟩}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨v,v⟩ & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (1\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =1+4+5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖v‖ & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo normalize the vector $v$, we divide $v$ by its norm $‖v‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{v}{‖v‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186147535 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385265 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186151814 1071 "What is the negation of the following statement?\n\n$$\n\\forall y \\in \\overline{S}, \\, \\neg P(y)\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186151814 1071 "What is the negation of the following statement?\n\n$\\forall y \\in \\overline{S}, \\, \\neg P(y)$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186151814 1074 "To negate the universal statement\n\n$$\n\\forall y \\in \\overline{S}, \\, \\neg P(y)\n$$\n\nwe flip the quantifier to existential and negate the predicate:\n\n$$\n¬(∀y ∈ S^(―), ¬P(y)) \\mid ≡∃y ∈ S^(―), ¬¬P(y); ≡∃y ∈ S^(―), P(y)\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186151814 1074 "To negate the universal statement $\\forall y \\in \\overline{S}, \\, \\neg P(y),$ we flip the quantifier to existential and negate the predicate: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀y\\in \\overset{―}{S},\\,¬P(y)) & ≡∃y\\in \\overset{―}{S},\\,¬¬P(y) \\\\  & ≡∃y\\in \\overset{―}{S},\\,P(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186151815 1071 "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ For all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, $n$ is a composite number." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186151815 1071 "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ For all $n \\in \\mathbb{N},$ $n$ is a composite number." 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186151815 1074 "If we denote\n\n![](images/b8/b8d152c4a9f69b2a6ea0657d9e66ca4adbc11bc480d25a28ea07eac28a646ac3.png)\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n\\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, P(n).\n$$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\neg \\left( \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, P(n)  \\right) \\equiv \\exists n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\neg P(n).\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\exists n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\neg P(n)$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ **There exists** $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that $n$ is **not** a composite number." 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186151815 1074 "If we denote\n\n$$\nn\n$$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n\\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, P(n)\n$$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\neg \\left(\\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, P(n) \\right) \\equiv \\exists n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\neg P(n)\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\exists n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\, \\neg P(n)\n$$\n\nreads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ **There exists** $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that $n$ is **not** a composite number." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186151816 1071 "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ Some quadrilaterals are squares." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186151816 1071 "Negate the following statement: \n\n$\\qquad$ Some quadrilaterals are squares." 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186151816 1074 "Let $Q$ be the set of all quadrilaterals and $S$ the set of all squares. The given statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ There exists $q \\in Q$ such that $q \\in S$\n\nIntroducing the existential quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\exists q \\in Q, \\, q \\in S$.\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n$¬(∃q ∈ Q, q ∈ S) \\mid ≡∀q ∈ Q, ¬(q ∈ S); ≡∀q ∈ Q, q ∉ S$\n\nwhich reads\n\n$\\qquad$ For all $q \\in Q$, $q \\not\\in S$.\n\nor simply\n\n$\\qquad$ All quadrilaterals are not squares." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186151816 1074 "Let $Q$ be the set of all quadrilaterals and $S$ the set of all squares. The given statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ There exists $q \\in Q$ such that $q \\in S$\n\nIntroducing the existential quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\exists q \\in Q, \\, q \\in S.$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∃q\\in Q,\\,q\\in S) & ≡∀q\\in Q,\\,¬(q\\in S) \\\\  & ≡∀q\\in Q,\\,q\\notin S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich reads \n\n$\\qquad$ For all $q \\in Q,$ $q \\not\\in S.$\n\nor simply\n\n$\\qquad$ All quadrilaterals are not squares." 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186151958 1071 "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\n- $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(x) = 1-x^2$\n\n- $g: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $g(x) = \\sin x$\n\n- $h: \\mathbb{R} \\to (0, \\infty)$ defined by $h(x) = 3^x$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186151958 1071 "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\n1. $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(x) = 1-x^2$\n2. $g: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $g(x) = \\sin x$\n3. $h: \\mathbb{R} \\to (0, \\infty)$ defined by $h(x) = 3^x$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186151958 1074 "Recall that a function $f: X \\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. Notice that $f(x)=1-x^2$ has two roots, $x=\\pm 1$. So, we have $f(-1)=0=f(1)$, while $-1 \\neq 1$.\n\n- II is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\sin x$ is $[-1,1]$. This means that $-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1$.\nSo, for example, there is no value $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $g(x) = 2$.\n\n- III is a bijection. First of all, $h$ is a surjection because $\\forall\\, y \\in (0, \\infty)$, we can choose an $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $h(x)=y$:\n$h(x)|= y; 3^{x}|= y; \\log_{3} (3^{x})|= \\log_{3} (y); x|= \\log_{3} (y)$\nLet's verify the above formula:\n$h(x) = h \\left(\\log_3\\left(y\\right) \\right) = 3^{\\log_3\\left(y\\right)} = y$\nSecond, $h$ is an injection:\n$h(x_{1})|= h(x_{2}); 3^{x_{1}}|= 3^{x_{2}}; \\log_{3} (3^{x_{1}})|= \\log_{3} (3^{x_{2}}); x_{1}|= x_{2}$\nBecause $h$ is both surjective and injective, we conclude that $h$ is bijective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186151958 1074 "Recall that a function $f: X \\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. Notice that $f(x)=1-x^2$ has two roots, $x=\\pm 1.$ So, we have $f(-1)=0=f(1),$ while $-1 \\neq 1.$\n\n- II is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\sin x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $g(x) = 2.$\n\n- III is a bijection. First of all, $h$ is a surjection because $\\forall\\, y \\in (0, \\infty),$ we can choose an $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $h(x)=y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(x) & =y \\\\ {3}^{x} & =y \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{x}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(y) \\\\ x & ={log}_{3}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify the above formula:\n\n$$\nh(x) = h \\left(\\log_3\\left(y\\right) \\right) = 3^{\\log_3\\left(y\\right)}  = y\n$$\n\nSecond, $h$ is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h({x}_{1}) & =h({x}_{2}) \\\\ {3}^{{x}_{1}} & ={3}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{{x}_{1}}) & ={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{{x}_{2}}) \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $h$ is both surjective and injective, we conclude that $h$ is bijective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186151959 1071 "![](images/fd/fd5dc7179dcd9f4262db3b3848eb6bb0b8d21700ab96af1dbe5d4d0b75ba7b5d.png)\n\n        \n\nFor the function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to [0,\\infty)$ defined by $f(x)=|x|,$ which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n\n- The function is a surjection\n\n- The function is a bijectiion" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186151959 1071 "![](images/fd/fd5dc7179dcd9f4262db3b3848eb6bb0b8d21700ab96af1dbe5d4d0b75ba7b5d.png)\n\nFor the function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to [0,\\infty)$ defined by $f(x)= \\mid x \\mid$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The function is an injection\n2. The function is a surjection\n3. The function is a bijectiion" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186151959 1074 "Recall that a function $f: X \\to Y$ is\n\n- an injection if $\\forall\\, x_1,x_2 \\in X$, whenever $f(x_1)=f(x_2)$, we have $x_1=x_2$,\n- a surjection if $\\forall\\, y \\in Y$, $\\exists\\, x \\in X$ such that $f(x)=y$,\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is *not* an injection, because there are horizontal lines (like the one depicted below) that cross the graph of $f(x)$ more than once. So, $\\exists\\, x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb R$ such that $x_1 \\neq x_2$ but $f(x_1)=f(x_2)$.\n\n![](images/9c/9c98a703475ea0153b309920ab2b3d9da5e625f7ccc456e377c3ea5e2feeb5c4.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is a surjection because, according to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ indeed covers the interval $[0, \\infty)$.\n- Statement III is false. The function is *not* a bijection because the function is not an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186151959 1074 "Recall that a function $f: X \\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $\\forall\\, x_1,x_2 \\in X,$ whenever $f(x_1)=f(x_2),$ we have $x_1=x_2,$\n\n- a surjection if $\\forall\\, y \\in Y,$ $\\exists\\, x \\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is *not* an injection, because there are horizontal lines (like the one depicted below) that cross the graph of $f(x)$ more than once. So, $\\exists\\, x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb R$ such that $x_1 \\neq x_2$ but $f(x_1)=f(x_2).$\n\n![](images/9c/9c98a703475ea0153b309920ab2b3d9da5e625f7ccc456e377c3ea5e2feeb5c4.png)\n\n       \n\n- Statement II is true. The function is a surjection because, according to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ indeed covers the interval $[0, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is *not* a bijection because the function is not an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152017 1071 "![](images/3a/3a2e86835240add2f2e268f69f20ac8a0e77d07d75dfd1dab824a04c338f6891.png)\n\n  \n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X \\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f$ is a surjection\n\n- $f$ is an injection\n\n- $f$ is a bijection" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152017 1071 "![](images/3a/3a2e86835240add2f2e268f69f20ac8a0e77d07d75dfd1dab824a04c338f6891.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X \\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n1. $f$ is a surjection\n2. $f$ is an injection\n3. $f$ is a bijection" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186152017 1074 "Recall that a function $f: X \\to Y$ is\n\n- a surjection if $\\forall\\, y \\in Y$, $\\exists\\, x \\in X$ such that $f(x)=y$,\n- an injection if $\\forall\\, x_1,x_2 \\in X$, whenever $f(x_1)=f(x_2)$, we have $x_1=x_2$,\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the diagram, for every element $y \\in Y$, we have an arrow from some element $x \\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y$. So, $f$ is a surjection.\n- Statement II is false. We have $f(4)=f(6)$ but $4 \\neq 6$. So, it's *not true* that for all $x_1$ and $x_2$ contained within the domain $X$, if $f(x_1)$ and $f(x_2)$ are equal, then we must have $x_1=x_2$. As a result, $f$ is *not* an injection.\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186152017 1074 "Recall that a function $f: X \\to Y$ is \n\n- a surjection if $\\forall\\, y \\in Y,$ $\\exists\\, x \\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- an injection if $\\forall\\, x_1,x_2 \\in X,$ whenever $f(x_1)=f(x_2),$ we have $x_1=x_2,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the diagram, for every element $y \\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x \\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$ So, $f$ is a surjection.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $f(4)=f(6)$ but $4 \\neq 6.$ So, it's *not true* that for all $x_1$ and $x_2$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f(x_1)$ and $f(x_2)$ are equal, then we must have $x_1=x_2.$ As a result, $f$ is *not* an injection.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152018 1071 "![](images/98/98ba885ed52b33359898e7736a5913a0570cf2d158a657475b9541cc271245e9.png)\n\n \n\nFor the function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(x)=\\dfrac{4}{x^2+1},$ which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n\n- The function is a surjection\n\n- The function is a bijection" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152018 1071 "![](images/98/98ba885ed52b33359898e7736a5913a0570cf2d158a657475b9541cc271245e9.png)\n\nFor the function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(x)=\\dfrac{4}{x^2+1}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The function is an injection\n2. The function is a surjection\n3. The function is a bijection" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186152018 1074 "Recall that a function $f: X \\to Y$ is\n\n- an injection if $\\forall\\, x_1,x_2 \\in X$, whenever $f(x_1)=f(x_2)$, we have $x_1=x_2$,\n- a su