 17592186382812 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382819 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385795 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385802 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152270 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382791 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152270 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382798 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152270 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382805 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152270 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385816 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382784 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385637 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385665 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152271 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385809 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149189 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186152448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186383627 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152448 1071 "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ There exists $x\\in Z$ such that $x<4.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152448 1071 "Negate the following statement:\n\nThere exists $x ∈ Z$ such that $x < 4$." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186152448 1074 "If we denote\n\n$$\nP(x):x<4,\n$$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in Z,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n¬(∃x\\in Z,\\,P(x))≡∀x\\in Z,\\,¬P(x).\n$$\n\nFinally, $∀x\\in Z,\\,¬P(x)$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **For all** $x\\in Z,$ $x\\text{\\boldsymbol}\\ge 4.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150899 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186152737 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385612 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152737 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152737 1071 "For the function $f: R → R$ defined by $f(x) = x^{2}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The function is an injection\n2. The function is a surjection\n3. The function is a bijection" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186152737 1071 "For the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{2},$ which of the following  statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152737 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. For instance, $f(2)=4=f(-2)$, but $2\\ne -2.$ So, $f(x)$ is not an injection.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, there is no $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$ So, $f(x)$ is not a surjection.\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not both an injection and a surjection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150929 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186152742 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385625 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152742 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152742 1071 "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\n- $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}-2$\n- $h:Q\\to Q$ defined by $h(x)=8x-3$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152742 1071 "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n\n1. $f: R → R$ defined by $f(x) = \\cos x$\n2. $g: Q → Q$ defined by $g(x) = x^{2} - 2$\n3. $h: Q → Q$ defined by $h(x) = 8x - 3$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186152742 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=2.$\n\n- II is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. Notice that $g$ has two roots, $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ So, we have $g(-\\sqrt{2})=0=g(\\sqrt{2}),$ while $-\\sqrt{2}\\ne \\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- III is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in Q,$ we can choose an $x\\in Q$ such that $h(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(x) & =y \\\\ 8x-3 & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y+3}{8}\\in Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\nh(x)=h(\\frac{y+3}{8})=8(\\frac{y+3}{8})-3=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h({x}_{1}) & =h({x}_{2}) \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-3 & =8{x}_{2}-3 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $h$ is both surjective and injective, $h$ is bijective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152743 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150935 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186152743 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385631 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152743 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152743 1071 "![](images/06/06e3c48955dc8f907c87a93aeaa7c2a7240ea194fe1ca6a96b2a0bf566882264.png)\n\nFor the function $f: R → [0, ∞)$ defined by $f(x) = 0.25x^{2}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The function is an injection\n2. The function is a surjection\n3. The function is a bijection" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186152743 1071 "![](images/06/06e3c48955dc8f907c87a93aeaa7c2a7240ea194fe1ca6a96b2a0bf566882264.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)=0.25{x}^{2}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152743 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. There are horizontal lines (like the one depicted below) that cross the graph of $f(x)$ more than once. So, $∃\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that ${x}_{1}\\ne {x}_{2}$ but $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}).$\n\n![](images/fc/fce55ada0375bff9bd3ea5852e80f707d70f145211b60e80b5a16b3049020000.png)\n\n          \n\n- Statement II is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ indeed covers the interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151565 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186152848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385768 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152848 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152848 1071 "![](images/51/5130380c907723f29eb6232b5de8ea1fb93716c20df983a47725cb3e29e71702.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f: X → Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n1. $f$ is a surjection\n2. $f$ is an injection\n3. $f$ is a bijection" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186152848 1071 "![](images/51/5130380c907723f29eb6232b5de8ea1fb93716c20df983a47725cb3e29e71702.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n- $f$ is a surjection\n- $f$ is an injection\n- $f$ is a bijection" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186152848 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, according to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$ So, $f$ is a surjection.\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ As a result, $f$ is an injection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since both statements I and II are true, $f$ is a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186157072 1071 "Let $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ If $x < 0$, then $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_$.\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is trivial?\n\n1. $\\pi < 3$\n2. $2x+1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$\n3. $\\mid x \\mid \\geq 0$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186157072 1071 "Let $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ If $x < 0,$ then $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is trivial?\n\n- $\\pi < 3$\n\n- $2x+1 \\in \\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $|x| \\geq 0$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186157072 1074 "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP \\quad \\Rightarrow \\overbrace{{\\color{blue}Q}}^{\\textrm{Always true}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our consequents in turn.\n\n- Statement I is always false. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Predicate II, defined for $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ is *not* always true. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ is always true. $\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186157072 1074 "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP \\quad \\Rightarrow \\overbrace{{\\color{blue}Q}}^{\\textrm{Always true}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our consequents in turn.\n\n- Statement I is always false. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Predicate II, defined for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, is *not* always true. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- Predicate III, defined for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, is always true. $\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186157073 1071 "Let $n\\in\\mathbb{N}$. Prove that if $n\\gt 4$, then $n^2 + \\dfrac{1}{n} > 1$." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186157073 1071 "Let $n\\in\\mathbb{N}.$ Prove that if $n\\gt 4,$ then $n^2 + \\dfrac{1}{n} > 1.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186157073 1074 "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP \\quad \\Rightarrow \\overbrace{{\\color{blue}Q}}^{\\textrm{Always true}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, we proceed as follows:\n\nThe theorem can be written as the implication \n\n$$\nn\\gt 4\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\nn^2 + \\dfrac{1}{n} > 1.\n$$\n\nNotice that for $n\\in\\mathbb{N},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+\\frac{1}{n} & >{n}^{2} \\\\  & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following fact:\n\nThe consequent $n^2 + \\dfrac{1}{n} > 1$ of our implication is always true.\n\nFinally, we can make the following conclusion:\n\nTherefore, the theorem is trivially true." 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186157073 1074 "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n$P \\quad \\Rightarrow \\overbrace{{\\color{blue}Q}}^{\\textrm{Always true}}$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, we proceed as follows:\n\n> The theorem can be written as the implication\n> $n\\gt 4 \\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad n^2 + \\dfrac{1}{n} > 1$.\n\nNotice that for $n\\in\\mathbb{N}$, we have\n\n$$\nn^{2} + \\frac{1}{n} \\mid > n^{2}; > 1\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following fact:\n\n> The consequent $n^2 + \\dfrac{1}{n} > 1$ of our implication is always true.\n\nFinally, we can make the following conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, the theorem is trivially true." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186157074 1071 "Let $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_$, then $\\mid \\tan x \\mid < 1$.\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is vacuous?\n\n1. $\\cos{\\pi}= -1$\n2. $(x+1)^2 \\ge 3$\n3. $\\mid \\sin{x} \\mid > 1$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186157074 1071 "Let $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_,$ then $|\\tan x| < 1.$ \n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is vacuous?\n\n- $\\cos{\\pi}= -1$\n\n- $(x+1)^2 \\ge 3$\n\n- $|\\sin{x}| > 1$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186157074 1074 "We say that an implication has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{{\\color{blue}P}}^{\\textrm{Always false}} \\Rightarrow \\quad Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter wh