| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=1)=f(1,1)=0.6.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.75\\cdot 0.8=0.6.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.05=0.25\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,0) & =0.15=0.75\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(0,1) & =0.20=0.25\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186166753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383613 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186166753 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385844 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186166755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385369 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186166755 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385830 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186166756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383620 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186166756 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385376 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186166805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385892 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186166807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186383458 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186166852 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382688 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186166852 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382695 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186166852 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382702 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186166852 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382709 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186166860 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382977 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186166865 1038 #edb/ref 17592186382963 13194139533455 17592186045585