$\\text{_________}\\,.$*" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186184769 1074 "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$C$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to function addition and scalar multiplication of a function by a real number) because **multiplication of $f\\in C$ by a scalar is not closed.** *\n\nFor example, if $\\alpha =-1$ and $f(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ then $\\alpha f(x)=-f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nOn the other hand:\n\n- Addition of $f,g\\in C$ is associative. $\\,✓$\n\n- $C$ contains the zero element. Indeed, the function $e(x),$ such that $e(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R,$ belongs to $C.$ This function is the zero element of $C$ since $e(x)+f(x)=f(x)+e(x)=f(x)$ for any $f(x)\\in C.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in C.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- Addition of $f,g\\in C$ is commutative. $\\,✓$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186198018 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n![](images/14/149438cd70059230993ca93a2122158f71edb665b3948cbd4ff3a9c038d7e48f.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $u(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ and $v(x,y) = e^{x-y}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & ={e}^{x-y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, t