33 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186198622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186383541 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186198622 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186198622 1071 "Let $f: U → R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a) = b$ and $f^{′}(a) \\ne 0$ for $a ∈ U$. Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n1. The function $f$ is invertible on $U$.\n2. The inverse $f^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b$.\n3. The derivative of $f^{-1}$ is given by $(f^{-1})^{′}(b) =-f^{′}(a)$." 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186198622 1071 "Let $f:U\\to R$ be continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R$ such that $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U.$ Which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- The function $f$ is invertible on $U.$\n- The inverse ${f}^{-1}$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n- The derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=-{f}^{′}(a).$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186198622 1074 "The inverse function theorem for single-variable functions states the following:\n\n*If $f:U\\to R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U⊆R,$ $f(a)=b$ and ${f}^{′}(a)\\ne 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n\n- *${f}^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n\n- *the derivative of ${f}^{-1}$ is given by* \n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(b))}\\,.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the theorem, the function $f$ must be invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$ which could be smaller than $U.$ \n\nFor example, the function $f(x)={x}^{2}$ satisfies the conditions of the inverse function theorem for $U=(-2,2)$ and $a=1.$ However, $f(x)$ is not invertible on the entire set $U,$ only on a subset of $U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the inverse ${f}^{-1}$ must be continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the theorem, we must have ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(b)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(a)}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186217252 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385282 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186217256 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385236 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186221425 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385228 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186221425 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385247 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186229136 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385211 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186229167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385184 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186229181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385255 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186229189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385214 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186229189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385221 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186231412 1038 #edb/ref 17592186385302 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186231579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186230313 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186231579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385297 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186231579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186231579 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $1$-periodic and odd. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186231579 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n$y^{″} + 2y = f(x), y(0) = 0, y(1) = 3$\nwhere $f(x)$ is $1$-periodic and odd. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution $y_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution $y_{p}$ satisfies $y_{p}(0) = 0$ and $y_{p}(1) = 0$.*\n\n$(3)/(\\cos (2))\\cos (2πx)$ {{field-1}}" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186231579 1074 "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+2y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=3$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 3 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186259741 1073 true 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186281354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281151 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186281354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186383547 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186308528 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{4}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 false] [17592186308528 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186382689 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382688 1069 "q-104400" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382688 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382688 1071 "For which of the following vector fields is $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)$ a potential function?" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382688 1072 #uuid "7a92703b-4103-5d43-a169-46ae386c0c50" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382688 1074 "A vector field $F$ is conservative if it is the gradient of a scalar function $f.$ That is, there exists a function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ The function $f$ is called a potential function for $F.$\n\nTo compute the vector field associated with a potential function $f,$ we compute its gradient.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1))\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\  & =F\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field that has $f$ as a potential function is\n\n$$\nF=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j.\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382689 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382690 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382689 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382691 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382689 1075 #edb/ref 