[17592186382726 1081 "III only" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382726 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382726 1084 #uuid "b43f392a-de66-542d-85c1-a5e456a297d9" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382727 1081 "II only" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382727 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382727 1084 #uuid "a163be0a-2b14-51bd-a9a8-1d4a4207b41d" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382728 1068 #edb/ref 17592186382729 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382728 1069 "q-105133" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382728 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382728 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=x+2?$\n\n- $f(-1)\\ne f(1)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is an injection" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382728 1072 #uuid "b421e9a7-e6c7-514b-9eac-4c4a22d0b15a" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382728 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/3b/3b849edf2ae8976e0abd5e0aa43180ad61d90b01d6f229c2b7fc7898ea860400.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $f(-1)=(-1)+2=1$ and $f(1)=1+2=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $R$ that map to the same element in $R.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n