c923e2215a7718d75c9c42e26.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382832 1072 #uuid "a2ed7472-9bdb-5c0c-a495-7db6294676ad" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382832 1074 "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a source at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P.$\n\n- If $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P,$ then $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statements II and III are false. For any point $P,$ the sum of the inward-pointing vectors equals the sum of the outward-pointing vectors. Therefore, $F$ acts neither like a source nor a sink.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere, the vector field $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382833 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382834 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382833 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382835 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382833 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382836 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382833 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382837 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382833 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382838 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382833 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382833 1077 "selection" 13194139533455 17592186