86 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382846 1071 "![](images/10/10a9960200e87e1b7b8a38789d5f70f29e48ad1f0a6e2913713da90c532eca6e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is not irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382846 1072 #uuid "73b73866-28ec-57e7-b02e-d1bd053c3918" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382846 1074 "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/59/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statements III and IV are false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational. And, since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382847 1075 #edb/ref 1759218638