39533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382970 1071 "![](images/32/32fefe70890d179803a847fc597349948703176d1561a8c840e845fa7a06a872.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+2}{x-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=(x-1)(y-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y-2}{x}$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382970 1072 #uuid "9198e7bb-f6e2-569d-9a58-c321fc238e98" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382970 1074 "![](images/a1/a18f69bf5b2cb6b2984722f851e1eda017e48863517da7ee65597d7c25c46379.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $x\\ne 1,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=-2.$\n\n- For $y\\ne -2,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. For $x\\ne 1,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+2}{x-1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=-2,$ and for $y\\ne -2,$\n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{y+2}{x-1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(x-1)(y-2)$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=-2$ (the value depends on $x$).\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y-2}{x}$ evaluates to zero on $y=-2,$ but \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{y-2}{x}|\\to |y-2|\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382971 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382972 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382971 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382973 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382971 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382974 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382971 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382975 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382971 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382976 13194139533455 1759218604