$O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382977 1072 #uuid "7832c005-e5d4-5bbe-91b5-55956ce35ed8" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382977 1074 "![](images/4e/4e0b8b816ca3b86fa90ea785df1308f53a4763baa2c2aa2192b9d7bfa0259855.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} & ={1}^{4}+2(1)^{2}+C \\\\ 3 & =3+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{2({2}^{4}+2(2)^{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186382978 1075 #edb/ref 17592186382979 13194139533455 17592186045585 t