499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383011 1084 #uuid "5fca2b8f-91e9-5173-9378-858ea3cdc4f4" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383012 1068 #edb/ref 17592186383013 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383012 1069 "q-122687" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383012 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383012 1071 "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2}?$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383012 1072 #uuid "1b63c956-bea1-5b1b-a3bb-61d243984ef7" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383012 1074 "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =x\\,i-y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & =(-2)\\,i-j=-2\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =i-j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\,i-(-1)\\,j=2i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/fd/fdcd6cebad2e9aef26daf2987b2890a1b05ceeab5f8e803b631cdb4a6a4598c4.png)" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383013 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383014 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383013 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383015 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383013 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383016 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383013 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383017 13194139533455 175921860455