383152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383152 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=-x{y}^{2}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1).$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383152 1072 #uuid "842a0b51-a8a6-5106-93ae-c9c478945bc0" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383152 1074 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,-1)=-1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =-{y}^{2}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(1,-1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =-2xy &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(1,-1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨-1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1-(x-1)+2(y+1) \\\\  & ≈2-x+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383153 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383154 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383153 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383155 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383153 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383156 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383153 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383157 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383153 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383158 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383153 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383153 1077 "selection" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383153 1079 #edb/ref 17592186383158 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383153 1080 #uuid "f102ebcb-0a48-5085-94bc-c1ac0bda4076" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [175921