educe that ${\\sigma }_{1}=15$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=10.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=225,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-225I & =[\\begin{aligned}180 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 145\\end{aligned}]-225[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-45 & -60 \\\\ -60 & -80\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-225I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=100,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-100I & =[\\begin{aligned}180 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 145\\end{aligned}]-100[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}80 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 45\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-100I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A:$ Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\end