nts.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=1,Y=2)=f(1,2)=0.15.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=1)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.25\\cdot 0.6=0.15.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.1=0.25\\cdot 0.4={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(2,1) & =0.3=0.75\\cdot 0.4={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(2,2) & =0.45=0.75\\cdot 0.6={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383473 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383474 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383473 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383475 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383473 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383476 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383473 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383477 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383473 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383478 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383473 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383473 1077 "selection" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383473 1079 #edb/ref 17592186383474 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383473 1080 #uuid "57688bd7-0235-509e-9acb-ed7f12697513" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383474 1081 "I, II, and III" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383474 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383474 1084 #uuid "00b166a9-fe42-5e03-99fb-bbc0b4fa6e63" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383475 1081 "I and III only" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383475 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383475 1084 #uuid "50bb9299-0cb5-5ae3-9e68-b8daa73f