ef 17592186045487 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383620 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=z-x-y,$ where $x={e}^{s}+t,$ $y=s+1$ and $z=1-t-s.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383620 1072 #uuid "04777d40-e9e1-5da3-94ee-34ffd4874f77" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383620 1074 "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & ={e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(-1)\\cdot {e}^{s}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-{e}^{s}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383621 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383622 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383621 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383623 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383621 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383624 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383621 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383625 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383621 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383626 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383621 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383621 1077 "selection" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383621 1079 #edb/ref 17592186383624 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [1759218