$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$P(n):\\,3\\,∣\\,{n}^{3}-7n+9$ for all $n\\in N$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into the expression and show it is divisible by $3:$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$ Substituting $n=1$ into the expression ${n}^{3}-7n+9,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(1):\\,{1}^{3}-7(1)+9=3,\\,\\text{and}\\,3\\,∣\\,3\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(k):\\,3\\,∣\\,{k}^{3}-7k+9.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\nP(k+1):\\,3\\,∣\\,(k+1)^{3}-7(k+1)+9.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the expression $(k+1)^{3}-7(k+1)+9$ as ${k}^{3}-7k+9$ (from the inductive hypothesis) plus a multiple of $3.$ We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{3}-7(k+1)+9 & ={k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+3k+1-7k-7+9 \\\\  & ={k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}-4k+3 \\\\  & =({k}^{3}-7k+9)+3({k}^{2}+k-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $3\\,∣\\,({k}^{3}-7k+9)$ by the inductive hypothesis, and $3({k}^{2}+k-2)$ is clearly divisible by $3,$ their sum is also divisible by $3,$ as required.\n\nSince we have shown that the expression in $(∗)$ is divisible by $3,$ our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $3\\,∣\\,{n}^{3}-7n+9$ for all $n\\in N.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383953 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383954 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186383