id.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(k):\\,7\\,∣\\,{2}^{3k}+{15}^{k}-2.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\nP(k+1):\\,7\\,∣\\,{2}^{3(k+1)}+{15}^{k+1}-2.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the expression ${2}^{3(k+1)}+{15}^{k+1}-2$ as ${2}^{3k}+{15}^{k}-2$ (taken from the inductive hypothesis) plus a multiple of $7.$ We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3(k+1)}+{15}^{k+1}-2 & ={2}^{3k}\\cdot {2}^{3}+{15}^{k}\\cdot 15-2 \\\\  & =8({2}^{3k})+15({15}^{k})-2 \\\\  & =7({2}^{3k})+14({15}^{k})+{2}^{3k}+{15}^{k}-2 \\\\  & =7({2}^{3k})+14({15}^{k})+({2}^{3k}+{15}^{k}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $7\\,∣\\,({2}^{3k}+{15}^{k}-2)$ by the inductive hypothesis, and $7({2}^{3k})$ and $14({15}^{k})=7\\cdot 2({15}^{k})$ are both clearly divisible by $7,$ their sum is also divisible by $7,$ as required.\n\nSince we have shown that the expression in $(∗)$ is divisible by $7,$ our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $7\\,∣\\,{2}^{3n}+{15}^{n}-2$ for all $n\\in N.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384157 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384158 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384157 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384159 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384157 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384160 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384157 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384157 1077 "field-3" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384157 1079 #edb/ref 17592186384159 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384157 1080 #uuid "03cae898-9f98-529e-81d0-6b0ba6ff7b0d" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384158 1081 "{2}^{3(1)}-{15}^{1}+2" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384158 1083 #edb/ref 1759218604549