e] [17592186384375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186384418 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186384423 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186384427 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186384430 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186384434 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186384438 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186384443 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186384448 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384375 1069 "q-183934" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384375 1071 "Given the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=8{a}_{n}-16{a}_{n-1},\\,{a}_{1}=0,\\,{a}_{2}=16,\\,n\\in N,\\,n\\ge 2\n$$\n\nuse strong induction to prove the following statement:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={4}^{n}(n-1),\\,∀n\\in N\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384375 1072 #uuid "13011db6-4bc4-5061-b981-fad630835e69" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384375 1074 "The principle of strong induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),$ $P(2),$ $P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true (as well as $P(2),P(3),P(4),…,$ if required), and\n\n- $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,{a}_{n}={4}^{n}(n-1)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base cases. Since the first application of the recurrence formula ${a}_{n+1}=8{a}_{n}-16{a}_{n-1}$ does not apply until ${a}_{3},$ we must check the base cases $P(1)$ and $P(2).$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove our statement is true for the base cases $n=1,2.$ \nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{4}^{1}(1-1)=0.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for $n=2,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=16,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{4}^{2}(2-1)=16.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\nNext, we write down our (strong) **inductive hypothesis,** which is to assume that $P(n)$ is true *for all* $n\\le k,$ where $k\\ge 2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={4}^{n}(n-1)$ for all $n\\le k$ for some $k\\ge 2.$ In particular, we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={4}^{k}(k-1) \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={4}^{k-1}(k-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the inductive step.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={4}^{k+1}k.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nUsing the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =8{a}_{k}-16{a}_{k-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{a}_{k}-16{a}_{k-1} & =8({4}^{k}(k-1))-16({4}^{k-1}(k-2)) \\\\  & ={4}^{k}(8k-8)-{4}^{k-1}(16k-32) \\\\  & ={4}^{k}(8k-8)-{4}^{k}(4k-8) \\\\  & ={4}^{k}(8k-8-4k+8) \\\\  & ={4}^{k+1}k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, by the principle of strong induction, ${a}_{n}={4}^{n}(n-1)$ is true for all $n\\in N.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384376 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384377 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384376 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384378 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384376 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384379 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384376 1