139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186384530 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384451 1069 "q-183945" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384451 1071 "Given the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=3{a}_{n}-2{a}_{n-1},\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=5,\\,n\\in N,\\,n\\ge 2\n$$\n\nuse strong induction to prove the following statement:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={2}^{n}+1,\\,∀n\\in N\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384451 1072 #uuid "a7139f52-ddf6-52e0-b88b-87f0c08ce99b" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384451 1074 "The principle of strong induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),$ $P(2),$ $P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true (as well as $P(2),P(3),P(4),…,$ if required), and\n\n- $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,{a}_{n}={2}^{n}+1\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base cases. Since the first application of the recurrence formula ${a}_{n+1}=3{a}_{n}-2{a}_{n-1}$ does not apply until ${a}_{3},$ we must check the base cases $P(1)$ and $P(2).$\n\n**Base Cases**: We first prove our statement is true for the base cases $n=1,2.$ \nFor $n=1,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=3,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{2}^{1}+1=3.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for $n=2,$ for the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=5,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{2}^{2}+1=5.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1$ and $n=2.$\n\nNext, we write down our (strong) **inductive hypothesis,** which is to assume that $P(n)$ is true *for all* $n\\le k,$ where $k\\ge 2.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that ${a}_{n}={2}^{n}+1$ for all $n\\le k$ for some $k\\ge 2.$ In particular, we have the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k} & ={2}^{k}+1 \\\\ {a}_{k-1} & ={2}^{k-1}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(1),P(2),…,P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the inductive step.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={2}^{k+1}+1.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nUsing the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =3{a}_{k}-2{a}_{k-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{k}-2{a}_{k-1} & =3({2}^{k}+1)-2({2}^{k-1}+1) \\\\  & =3({2}^{k})+3(1)-2({2}^{k-1})-2(1) \\\\  & =3({2}^{k})+3-{2}^{k}-2 \\\\  & =2({2}^{k})+1 \\\\  & ={2}^{k+1}+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, by the principle of strong induction, ${a}_{n}={2}^{n}+1$ is true for all $n\\in N.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384452 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384453 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384452 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384454 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384452 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384455 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384452 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384456 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384452 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384452 1077 "field-2" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384452 1079 #edb/ref 17592186384453 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384452 1080 #uuid "72f789a2-ba66-598b-bec5-f418c77568ff" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384453 1081 "3" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384453 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384453 1084 #uuid "fcbcc6bf-933d-521c