7592186384552 1071 "Given the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}={a}_{n}+4,\\,{a}_{1}=7,\\,n\\in N,\n$$\n\nuse mathematical induction to prove the following statement: \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=4n+3,\\,n\\in N\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384552 1072 #uuid "a4e25a91-ec55-5dff-8cb9-a8d3709f375d" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384552 1074 "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),$ $P(2),$ $P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\nP(n):\\,{a}_{n}=4n+3\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case. To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$ For the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=7,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n4(1)+3=7.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}=4k+3.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the inductive step.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =4(k+1)+3 \\\\  & =4k+7.\\,\\,(∗)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nUsing the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & ={a}_{k}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k}+4 & =(4k+3)+4 \\\\  & =4k+3+4 \\\\  & =4k+7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, by the principle of induction, ${a}_{n}=4n+3$ is true for all $n\\in N.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384553 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384554 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384553 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384555 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384553 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384556 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384553 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384553 1077 "field-4" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384553 1079 #edb/ref 17592186384554 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384553 1080 #uuid "d5295c91-4ba7-57e5-af15-7875130a7272" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384554 1081 "7" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384554 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384554 1084 #uuid "174c705e-d07e-5dba-a9e2-2617f4bd1181" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384555 1081 "1" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384555 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384555 1084 #uuid "f8f6bafc-d3d4-50c0-9500-e6f1e2791793" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384556 1081 "4" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384556 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384556 1084 #uuid "c82e7a32-1b22-5ea1-8ad3-6dca3d5f9499" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384558 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384559 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384560 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384561 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384557 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384557 107