o both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$ For the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1,\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{4}^{1}-3=1.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{k}={4}^{k}-3.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the inductive step.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{a}_{k+1}={4}^{k+1}-3.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ using the recurrence relation and inductive hypothesis to show that this equals the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nUsing the recurrence relation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{k+1} & =4{a}_{k}+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{k}+9 & =4({4}^{k}-3)+9 \\\\  & =4\\cdot {4}^{k}-12+9 \\\\  & ={4}^{k+1}-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, by the principle of induction, ${a}_{n}={4}^{n}-3$ is true for all $n\\in N.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384608 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384609 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384608 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384610 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384608 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384611 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384608 1075 #edb/ref 17592186384612 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384608 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384608 1077 "field-7" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384608 1079 #edb/ref 17592186384609 13194139533455 17592