85 true] [17592186384999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385004 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385007 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385012 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385016 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385022 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385028 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385033 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385038 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384999 1069 "q-217057" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384999 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384999 1071 "Prove that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}4(i-1)i(i+1)=(n-1)n(n+1)(n+2)$ using mathematical induction." 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384999 1072 #uuid "c8f997f6-7e5a-56a5-beec-d854b1bc1154" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186384999 1074 "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}4(i-1)i(i+1)=(n-1)n(n+1)(n+2)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}4(i-1)i(i+1)=4(0)(1)(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)(1)(2)(3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ \n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}4(i-1)i(i+1)=(k-1)k(k+1)(k+2).\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}4(i-1)i(i+1)=k(k+1)(k+2)(k+3).\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}4(i-1)i(i+1)=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}4(i-1)i(i+1))+4k(k+1)(k+2).\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}4(i-1)i(i+1))+4k(k+1)(k+2) \\\\  & =(k-1)k(k+1)(k+2)+4k(k+1)(k+2) \\\\  & =k(k+1)(k+2)((k-1)+4) \\\\  & =k(k+1)(k+2)(k+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}4(i-1)i(i+1)=(n-1)n(n+1)(n+2)$ is true for all $n\\ge 1.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385000 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385001 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385000 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385002 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385000 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385003 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186