d-0346dfba0d45" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385161 1074 "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{12}{{8}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}12\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{8}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=12\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=12\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{1}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2},r=\\frac{1}{8}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}12\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{4096})}{\\frac{7}{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{7}\\cdot \\frac{4095}{4096} \\\\  & =\\frac{1755}{1024}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385162 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385163 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385162 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385162 1077 "field-1" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385162 1079 #edb/ref 17592186385163 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385162 1080 #uuid "1fdb2fbd-dc7c-5f58-a46a-ea11565684c9" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385163 1081 "\\frac{1755}{1024}" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385163 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385163 1084 #uuid "67e99211-9be6-5336-8e27-d19d9b964287" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385165 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385164 1069 "q-277893" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385164 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $4$ and $9$, respectively,  the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385164 1072 #uuid "40ab7668-409e-5ae4-b596-91292edca0a1" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385164 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $4,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{4}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $9,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{9}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{4}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{9}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{9}={x}^{4+9}={x}^{13}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $13.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385165 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385166 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385165 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385165 1077 "field-1" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385165 1079 #edb/ref 17592186385166 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385165 1080 #uuid "6421989e-ec9d-59bb-9107-90585e4ce4a4" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385166 1081 "13" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385166 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385166 1084 #uuid "35aea64f-2981-55b6-9c9a-021b62b49a69" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385168 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385167 1069 "q-280153" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385167 1071 "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}10{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385167 1072 #uuid "23811638-4c1f-5bbb-8f02-a9310ea0d5d5" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385167 1074 "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out the value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=10{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we can calculate the first term ${a}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =10{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{5}{2},r=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}10{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{4})}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{256})}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{(\\frac{255}{256})}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{425}{128}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385168 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385169 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385168 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385168 1077 "field-1" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385168 1079 #edb/ref 17592186385169 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385168 1080 #uuid "a8ea3121-be54-5431-9f27-420ce42ace5f" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385169 1081 "\\frac{425}{128}" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385169 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385169 1084 #uuid "c9eee0e3-9af5-54fb-980b-f40dc6f09153" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385171 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385170 1069 "q-280295" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385170 1071 "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=0}^{3}\\frac{1}{3}{(6)}^{n-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385170 1072 #uuid "a5de08fc-5e0f-5332-a12e-e8d873f20db2" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385170 1074 "Since the given series is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1.$\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{0}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=6.$ Also, note that $N=4,$ since there are $4$ terms in total.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{3}{(6)}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nand so the first term ${a}_{0}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{3}{(6)}^{0-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{(6)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{18},r=6$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{6}^{4})}{(1-6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot \\frac{(1-1296)}{(-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1295}{90} \\\\  & =\\frac{259}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385171 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385172 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385171 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385171 1077 "field-1" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385171 1079 #edb/ref 17592186385172 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385171 1080 #uuid "db9b616d-d4f2-53e8-a4da-257c8430e1b3" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385172 1081 "\\frac{259}{18}" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385172 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385172 1084 #uuid "82227fc4-f71b-5e47-b6d5-bc7284151df4" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385174 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385173 1069 "q-316316" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385173 1071 "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=⟨2,4⟩$ and $\\text{v}=⟨1,a⟩$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385173 1072 #uuid "cbab4261-a7b7-5038-82e3-1c16585246bf" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385173 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & a\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & a\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot a-1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2a-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-4 & =0 \\\\ 2a & =4 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385174 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385175 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385174 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385174 1077 "field-1" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385174 1079 #edb/ref 17592186385175 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385174 1080 #uuid "c51475f4-4479-5e0c-82f3-531ab72e5ece" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385175 1081 "2" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385175 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385175 1084 #uuid "1056e378-d24a-52e1-8f1c-c7347c129259" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385177 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385176 1069 "q-320549" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385176 1071 "Given that the area spanned between the vectors $\\text{u}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $\\text{v}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ equals zero, what is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385176 1072 #uuid "429fe8f8-3824-533b-9d1a-5e0e8340c4c0" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385176 1074 "First, we construct a matrix where the columns are made up of the components of the corresponding vectors:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}7 & a \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, we find the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}7 & a \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =7\\cdot 5-a\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =35-\\frac{5a}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting the expression for the determinant equal to $0$ and solving for $a,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}35-\\frac{5a}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5a}{2} & =35 \\\\ 5a & =70 \\\\ a & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385177 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385178 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385177 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385177 1077 "field-1" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385177 1079 #edb/ref 17592186385178 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385177 1080 #uuid "e5ad92f1-0bf0-5997-8d7b-80739f643b06" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385178 1081 "14" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385178 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385178 1084 #uuid "a4c6ca74-2caa-55ff-9444-6529e60d11c7" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385180 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385182 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385179 1069 "q-333126" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385179 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)-8,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}=-\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{2}{s},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}=\\frac{2}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s-2}-\\frac{2}{s}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385179 1072 #uuid "a50de713-0586-573c-abb1-1467e2c93305" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385179 1074 "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{X}_{1}(s)} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{2}{s}} \\\\  & =-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+1}}+2\\,{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1\\cdot t}+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}{{X}_{2}(s)} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s-2}-\\frac{2}{s}} \\\\  & =2\\,{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+1}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-2}}-2\\,{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{-1\\cdot t}-{e}^{2\\cdot t}-2 \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}-{e}^{2t}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2-{e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-t}-{e}^{2t}-2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385180 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385181 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385180 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385180 1077 "field-1" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385180 1079 #edb/ref 17592186385181 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385180 1080 #uuid "2421d545-141b-5572-b6bb-c19167d055b6" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385181 1081 "2-{e}^{-t}" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385181 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385181 1084 #uuid "58e4f684-8df1-5f44-bccc-5c9dcc44dd9c" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385182 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385183 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385182 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385182 1077 "field-2" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385182 1079 #edb/ref 17592186385183 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385182 1080 #uuid "9c05ea1b-b283-55e0-bab0-2ff555ef073b" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385183 1081 "2{e}^{-t}-{e}^{2t}-2" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385183 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385183 1084 #uuid "861ca5eb-519b-5cd5-ac5c-1b66256d6d3f" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385185 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385184 1069 "q-334241" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385184 1071 "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.1$\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.8$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n- $\\lambda Δx=5$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385184 1072 #uuid "d7019503-fd19-5657-a7e0-238936f56d67" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385184 1074 "A numerical method \n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.1)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.1}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.1}| \\\\  & =0.9090…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.8)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.8}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.8}| \\\\  & =0.5555…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.2}| \\\\  & =0.4545…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+5}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{6}| \\\\  & =0.1666…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385185 1