e integrating factor is $I(y)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is {{field-2}}, where $c$ is a constant." 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385236 1072 #uuid "91806f1a-e750-5e85-a659-666c818f545f" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385236 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x,y) & ={e}^{-\\int 3/y\\text{ }\\text{d}y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}(xy\\,\\text{d}x-({x}^{2}+3{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ {{(\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}})}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+{{(-1)(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}+3)}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-3.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should inclu