 17592186385303 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385302 1069 "q-344315" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385302 1071 "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=4\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $1$-periodic and odd. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385302 1072 #uuid "a82ffc50-94c7-5ef3-8250-c59c884b5c7d" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385302 1074 "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+5y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=4$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 4 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385303 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385304 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385303 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385305 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385303 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385306 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385303 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385307 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385303 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385303 1077 "field-1" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385303 1079 #edb/ref 17592186385304 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385303 1080 #uuid "18f1e980-970f-59b0-bcb4-049b47db24a1" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385304 1081 "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385304 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385304 1084 #uuid "404cefb8-00ef-5c98-acc0-e0915a58fc3b" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385305 1081 "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385305 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385305 1084 #uuid "42cbf34b-8851-50bb-874f-4cdbdc1efcba" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385306 1081 "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385306 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385306 1084 #uuid "439c3659-0dc0-5024-9dc2-ea98ebfc52bf" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385307 1081 "\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385307 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385307 1084 #uuid "81883b38-c06e-5fce-ac3e-1c8abeb4e865" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385309 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385308 1069 "q-344843" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385308 1071 "$$\n{y}^{‴}+9{y}^{′}=-9\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x),\\,{{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x)=A+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{u}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{3}(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{2}(x)?$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385308 1072 #uuid "95c68a92-a31f-51d9-b3f4-ba9513a7793d" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385308 1074 "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=-9\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ 0 & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\ 0 & -9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\ -9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =27{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)-(-27{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =27{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+27{cos}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =27[{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)] \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{2},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{2}(x) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ 0 & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-0 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =(-1)^{3-2}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{2}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{-9\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{27}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385309 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385310 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385309 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385311 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385309 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385312 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385309 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385313 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385309 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385314 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385309 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 131