72 #uuid "93abda0d-dd4f-575f-bfb4-2c2551c6ca10" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385665 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. For example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=-1.$\n\n- II is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in Q,$ we can choose an $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =y \\\\ 4(x-2) & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y}{4}+2\\in Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\ng(x)=g(\\frac{y}{4}+2)=4([\\frac{y}{4}+2]-2)=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ 4({x}_{1}-2) & =4({x}_{2}-2) \\\\ {x}_{1}-2 & ={x}_{2}-2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is both surjective and injective, $g$ is bijective.\n\n- III is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x\\in Q$ such that $h(x)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385666 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385667 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385666 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385668 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385666 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385669 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385666 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385670 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385666 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385671 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385666 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385666 1077 "selection" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385666 1079 #edb/ref 17592186385671 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385666 1080 #uuid "06fb7149-2f4c-5ed2-99b2-de2082bfaad8" 13194139533455 17592186045585 true] [17592186385667 1081 "II and III only" 131